{title:'When Algorithms Enter the Classroom',topic:'Inteligência artificial · Educação',gloss:[['scaffold','temporary support for learning'],['proxy','indirect measure'],['bias','systematic distortion'],['granular','detailed at a fine level']],paras:[
'Artificial intelligence is entering classrooms through systems that recommend exercises, summarize discussions and identify students who may need additional support. Its appeal is understandable: a teacher responsible for dozens of learners cannot observe every misconception as it develops. An algorithm can compare a student’s recent work with thousands of previous responses and flag patterns that deserve attention. Such speed, however, does not by itself make an educational decision sound.',
'The most useful systems are not those that claim to know exactly what a learner should do next. They function more like scaffolds, offering teachers a granular view of recurring errors while leaving interpretation to a professional. A student who repeatedly omits a step in an equation may need conceptual instruction, whereas another student with the same answer pattern may simply have misunderstood the wording of a single question.',
'This distinction matters because educational data are proxies for learning, not learning itself. Time spent on a platform can reflect persistence, but it can also reflect a slow connection or a student who keeps an application open while working elsewhere. Likewise, a low score may indicate a missing prerequisite, unfamiliar language or an inaccessible interface. Treating a proxy as a direct measurement invites false precision.',
'Bias can enter at several stages. The historical examples used to train a model may reflect unequal access to technology. The labels assigned by teachers may vary across schools, and the platform may work better for the kinds of answers that its designers considered normal. Even when a model has no explicit demographic variable, correlated features can reproduce the same inequality. Removing a sensitive field therefore does not guarantee fairness.',
'Privacy creates a second set of questions. A school may legitimately need information to provide support, but collecting every click because it might be useful later expands the consequences of a data breach. Clear retention rules, restricted access and explanations that students can understand are not administrative details; they shape whether the technology is compatible with trust.',
'AI can therefore widen or narrow educational opportunity depending on the institutional choices surrounding it. Teachers need time to challenge an automated recommendation, students need ways to correct inaccurate records, and administrators need audits that examine outcomes rather than only technical accuracy. Used under those conditions, algorithms can extend human attention without pretending to replace human responsibility.'
